CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ assignment from a lambda: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ assignment from nullptr: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ copy assignment: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ move assignment: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
The parameter type `const function &' of an out-of-class definition did
not type-check (the injected-class-name), and an unchecked type matched
any member: the copy assignment's definition was attached to
`operator=(nullptr_t)' too.  libc++'s std::function defines its
assignment operators this way.
